--- body ---
## Context

`deliverables/_shared/_includes/clustering-comparison.md:51` ends its `full`
snapshot row with an HTML comment placed *after* the row's closing pipe:

    | full | v1.1 corpus (S2 + teaching expansion) | 27,315 | All works passing year/title filters | <!-- STALE: current analysis corpus is ~27,509; needs fresh compute_clustering_comparison run -->

The comment is a fifth cell against a four-column header, so the row renders
with a stray column. This is the same defect as ticket 0368's
`cop-topic-structure.md:38`, in a different file. Two instances make it a
habit rather than a slip: a provenance note appended to the row it annotates
instead of placed outside the table.

The note itself is worth keeping. It records that the snapshot sizes in this
table predate the current corpus, which is a real caveat on a rendered figure.
It just cannot sit past the closing pipe.

Found by running ticket 0340's `malformed_rows` parser over the hand-authored
includes. That sweep covers *generated* tables only, which is why a
hand-authored one went unnoticed until a guard was pointed at it by hand.

## Relevant files

- `deliverables/_shared/_includes/clustering-comparison.md:48-51` — the table
  and the offending row
- `deliverables/_shared/_includes/cop-topic-structure.md:38` — ticket 0368,
  the first instance; the two should be arbitrated together
- `tests/test_markdown_table_shape.py` — the parser that found it

## Actions

1. Move the STALE note out of the row: above the table, or below its last row
   with one blank line separating it.
2. Decide whether the note's substance still holds. It claims the table's
   27,315 predates a ~27,509 corpus and asks for a fresh
   `compute_clustering_comparison` run. If that run has happened, the fix is
   to update the numbers and retire the note.

## Test

Red first, alongside 0368's:

```python
def test_clustering_comparison_is_one_table():
    from test_markdown_table_shape import malformed_rows
    text = (REPO_ROOT / "deliverables/_shared/_includes/"
            "clustering-comparison.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    assert not [o for o in malformed_rows(text) if "cells, header" in o]
```

It reports `line 51: 5 cells, header at line 48 declares 4` today and nothing
after the fix.

The filter on `"cells, header"` is deliberate, and is itself the open question
below: the same file also reports a line-2 orphan, which is *not* a defect.
Line 2 is a hand-authored HTML comment containing a `|`, sitting outside every
table. Prose may carry a pipe; a generated table row may not.

## Verification

- [ ] `malformed_rows` reports no cell-count mismatch for the file
- [ ] The rendered PDF shows a two-row table with four columns, no stray cell
- [ ] The STALE provenance note survives in the source, or is deliberately
      retired because the numbers were refreshed

## Invariants

- No factual change to any snapshot size or construction description. This is
  a layout repair unless action 2 finds the numbers genuinely stale, in which
  case the refresh is a separate, sourced edit.
- `tests/test_markdown_table_shape.py` keeps its generated-only scope. Nothing
  here should make an adherence gate fail on hand-authored prose before the
  decision below is taken.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The table parses with every row at its declared width
- [ ] Taken together with 0368: a decision recorded on whether the 0340 sweep
      extends to `_includes/*.md`. Measured cost of extending it as-is: 9
      findings across 28 files — 2 real cell-count mismatches (this file and
      0368's) and 7 orphan reports, of which the line-2 ones are ordinary
      prose pipes in HTML comments and would need an exemption, not a fix.
